H=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;D=json.loads((H/'snapshot.json').read_text());Q={q[':db/id']:q for q in D['questions']};K={k[':db/id']:k for k in D['kps']};M=json.loads((H/'missing-correct.json').read_text());sys.path.insert(0,str(H.parent/'flagged'));from product_highlights import product_solution
solutions={
'q-10000':(17592186056082,r'Rearrange the equations so that each left side lists the coefficients of $x,y,z$ in that order:\n\n$$\n\left\{\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z&=-1\\4y+5z&=0\\x+6z&=3.\end{aligned}\right.\n$$\n\nMatching the indicated entries gives $a=0$, $b=5$, and $c=-1$.'),
'q-36184':(17592186058286,r'Each row of the coefficient matrix lists the coefficients of $x,y,z$ in the corresponding equation. The missing entries are the coefficient $5$ of $z$ in the first equation, the coefficient $8$ of $z$ in the second equation, and the coefficient $0$ of $x$ in the third equation. Thus $a=5$, $b=8$, and $c=0$.'),
'q-38128':(17592186058292,r'The matrix is diagonal, so its determinant is the product of its diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n2\cdot7\cdot(-3)=-42.\n$$'),
'q-9721':(17592186059333,r'The first equation is $1x+0y+0z=6$. The third equation rearranges to $-2x+0y+3z=2$. Matching these coefficients and constants with the matrix representation gives $a=1$, $b=0$, and $c=2$.'),
'q-9841':(17592186059354,r'Expanding across the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}\begin{vmatrix}0&-1&3\\2&0&1\\7&-1&0\end{vmatrix}&=0-(-1)\begin{vmatrix}2&1\\7&0\end{vmatrix}+3\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\7&-1\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-7)+3(-2)\\&=-13.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-7908':(17592186086851,r'Multiplying $A$ and $C$ in both orders gives\n\n$$\nAC=CA=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}=I_2.\n$$\n\nThus $C=A^{-1}$. Since $B\ne C$, statement III is false. Also\n\n$$\nAB=\begin{bmatrix}-1&1\\-2&0\end{bmatrix}\ne I_2,\n$$\n\nso statement I is false. Only statement II is true.'),
'q-78548':(17592186091107,r'The constant vector is $b=\begin{bmatrix}7\\-1\\-5\end{bmatrix}$. Multiplying by the given inverse gives\n\n$$\n\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}=A^{-1}b=\begin{bmatrix}1&0&2\\2&-2&3\\1&-1&1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}7\\-1\\-5\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}-3\\1\\3\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x+y+z=-3+1+3=1$.'),
'q-1029':(17592186097952,product_solution([[1,3],[0,-1]],[[3,1,2],[-1,0,4]],('X','Y'),[(i,j)for i in range(2)for j in range(3)])),
'q-1031':(17592186097958,r'The product of a $3\times1$ matrix and a $1\times2$ matrix is a $3\times2$ matrix. Multiply each entry of the column by each entry of the row:\n\n$$\n\begin{bmatrix}3\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}2&4\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}3\cdot2&3\cdot4\\0\cdot2&0\cdot4\\1\cdot2&1\cdot4\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}6&12\\0&0\\2&4\end{bmatrix}.\n$$'),
'q-1034':(17592186097962,product_solution([[5,1,3],[0,1,-2]],[[6,5],[9,8],[3,7]],('A','B'),[(i,j)for i in range(2)for j in range(2)])),
'q-13636':(17592186098176,r'The matrix $C$ has size $1\times2$, while $D$ has size $3\times1$. The number of columns in $C$ is $2$, and the number of rows in $D$ is $3$. These inner dimensions differ, so $CD$ is undefined.'),
'q-70791':(17592186100340,r'Multiply row $1$ of $A$ by column $3$ of $B$:\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}c_{13}&=\begin{bmatrix}-1&2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2\\5\end{bmatrix}\\&=(-1)(-2)+2(5)\\&=12.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-70792':(17592186100346,r'Multiply row $4$ of $X$ by column $2$ of $Y$:\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}z_{42}&=\begin{bmatrix}-5&6&-2\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-3\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}\\&=(-5)(-3)+6(0)+(-2)(1)\\&=13.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-20966':(17592186102772,None),
'q-35589':(17592186103889,r'The augmented matrix is\n\n$$\nM=\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1&0&2&2\\0&1&-3&1\\2&0&-4&-4\end{array}\right].\n$$\n\nApply $R_1:=-R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3-2R_1$. This gives the row echelon form\n\n$$\nM\sim\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&-2&-2\\0&1&-3&1\\0&0&0&0\end{array}\right].\n$$'),
'q-32040':(17592186113420,r'Let $A=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}$. Then\n\n$$\nEA=\begin{bmatrix}1&-2\\0&1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}a-2c&b-2d\\c&d\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe second row is unchanged, and the first row becomes the first row minus twice the second row. Therefore the operation is $R_1:=R_1+(-2)R_2$.'),
'q-32234':(17592186113476,r'The augmented matrix is\n\n$$\nM=\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&1&2&19\\3&1&2&31\end{array}\right].\n$$\n\nApply $R_2:=R_2-3R_1$, then $R_2:=-\frac12R_2$, and finally $R_1:=R_1-R_2$. The reduced row echelon form is\n\n$$\nM\sim\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&0&6\\0&1&2&13\end{array}\right].\n$$\n\nHence $x=6$ and $y=13-2z$, with $z$ free. The solution is\n\n$$\n\begin{bmatrix}6\\13-2z\\z\end{bmatrix},\qquad z\in(-\infty,\infty).\n$$'),
'q-42768':(17592186114006,r'The operation $R_1:=R_1-5R_2$ corresponds to left multiplication by $E_1=\begin{bmatrix}1&-5\\0&1\end{bmatrix}$. The operation $R_1:=3R_1$ corresponds to left multiplication by $E_2=\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}$. Because $E_1$ acts first, the required product is\n\n$$\nE_2E_1A=\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1&-5\\0&1\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$'),
'q-42810':(17592186114012,r'Apply the row operation to the identity matrix to obtain the elementary matrix\n\n$$\nE=\begin{bmatrix}-\frac14&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding operation on $A$ is left multiplication:\n\n$$\nEA=\begin{bmatrix}-\frac14&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$'),
'q-42812':(17592186114014,r'Let $A=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}$. Then\n\n$$\nEA=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\7&1\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\7a+c&7b+d\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe first row is unchanged, and the second row becomes the second row plus seven times the first row. Therefore the operation is $R_2:=R_2+7R_1$.'),
'q-42574':(17592186118719,r'Apply $R_1:=R_1+2R_4$, $R_2:=R_2+R_4$, and $R_3:=R_3-2R_4$. Adding a multiple of one row to another does not change the determinant, so it becomes\n\n$$\n\begin{vmatrix}5&0&1&-4\\-7&0&-2&2\\-11&0&-5&3\\2&1&4&-7\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nExpanding down column $2$, the only nonzero entry is $a_{42}=1$. Its cofactor sign is $(-1)^{4+2}=1$. Therefore the original determinant equals\n\n$$\n\begin{vmatrix}5&1&-4\\-7&-2&2\\-11&-5&3\end{vmatrix}.\n$$'),
'q-43062':(17592186118818,r'The determinant of a product equals the product of the determinants:\n\n$$\n\begin{aligned}\det(A)&=\begin{vmatrix}\sqrt3&-4\\-1&5\sqrt3\end{vmatrix}\begin{vmatrix}7&2\\5&3\end{vmatrix}\\&=(15-4)(21-10)\\&=11\cdot11\\&=121.\end{aligned}\n$$'),
'q-36304':(17592186183200,r'First compute\n\n$$\nB^2=\begin{bmatrix}-2&1\\-6&3\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}-2&1\\-6&3\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}4-6&-2+3\\12-18&-6+9\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}-2&1\\-6&3\end{bmatrix}=B.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ is idempotent, so every positive integer power equals $B$. In particular,\n\n$$\nB^5=\begin{bmatrix}-2&1\\-6&3\end{bmatrix}.\n$$')}
